<b> write once 

initialize to the real page with prot_none. Writer does this

Type* writer = PUBLISH_START(dataptr);

// modify writer

PUBLISH_END(writer);

============================================================================
Behind the scenes:
============================================================================
->  PUBLISH_START grabs a real lock to prevent multiple initializations.
    If bInitialized is FALSE return.

->  Creates a second mapping to dataptr's page with PROT_WRITE

->  Do the writes and stick a write barrier at the end

->  Release this mapping 

->  bInitialized = TRUE and release the real lock

->  Mark dataptr's page as PROT_READ

FAQ:
Q: What about cpus that are either not cache coherent or are weak coherent 
   meaning they require explicit read barriers to eliminate prefetched
   caches or stale cachelines of that address?
A: That shouldnt be a worry. Remember we are doing a mprotect that should
   cause TLB invalidates. On CPUs that are VIPT or PIPT the dcaches should
   repull data because of TLB change.

   Now for the VIVT dcaches like ARM v4/v5 remember linux kernel on a TLB
   invalidate does the dcache flush for you so we re safe here!!

Q: Err.. what does safe mean again?
A: Means doing the writer side write + fence + mprotect should guarantee
   no stale cachelines exist on any other core such that not having an
   explicit read barrier will cause problems!! WOOHOOO

   http://www.mjmwired.net/kernel/Documentation/cachetlb.txt
   explains it in details (look for Virtually indexed dcaches)



<c> Write Concurrently 
Simple do what you did with your Java implementation. 

Type* local = GETWRITER(original);

// modify local as often as you want **DO NOT PASS AROUND**

FLUSHWRITER(local); // This will take the real lock and merge local writer contents
		    // with main writer contents

Don't worry, if thread migrates OS is responsible for sticking in a write barrier.



